NNAAMMEE
     ttccppddrroopp — drop TCP connections

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ttccppddrroopp _l_o_c_a_l_-_a_d_d_r_e_s_s _l_o_c_a_l_-_p_o_r_t _f_o_r_e_i_g_n_-_a_d_d_r_e_s_s _f_o_r_e_i_g_n_-_p_o_r_t
     ttccppddrroopp [--ll] --aa
     ttccppddrroopp [--ll] --CC _c_c_-_a_l_g_o [--SS _s_t_a_c_k] [--ss _s_t_a_t_e]
     ttccppddrroopp [--ll] [--CC _c_c_-_a_l_g_o] --SS _s_t_a_c_k [--ss _s_t_a_t_e]
     ttccppddrroopp [--ll] [--CC _c_c_-_a_l_g_o] [--SS _s_t_a_c_k] --ss _s_t_a_t_e

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ttccppddrroopp command may be used to drop TCP connections from the command
     line.

     If --aa is specified then ttccppddrroopp will attempt to drop all TCP connections.

     If --CC _c_c_-_a_l_g_o is specified then ttccppddrroopp will attempt to drop all connec‐
     tions using the TCP congestion control algorithm _c_c_-_a_l_g_o.

     If --SS _s_t_a_c_k is specified then ttccppddrroopp will attempt to drop all connec‐
     tions using the TCP stack _s_t_a_c_k.

     If --ss _s_t_a_t_e is specified then ttccppddrroopp will attempt to drop all TCP con‐
     nections being in the state _s_t_a_t_e.  _s_t_a_t_e is one of SYN_SENT, SYN_RCVD,
     ESTABLISHED, CLOSE_WAIT, FIN_WAIT_1, CLOSING, LAST_ACK, FIN_WAIT_2, or
     TIME_WAIT.

     If multiple of --CC _c_c_-_a_l_g_o, --SS _s_t_a_c_k, and --ss _s_t_a_t_e are specified, ttccppddrroopp
     will attempt to drop all TCP connections using the congestion control al‐
     gorithm _c_c_-_a_l_g_o, being in the state _s_t_a_t_e, and using the TCP stack _s_t_a_c_k,
     if specified.  Since TCP connections in the TIME_WAIT state are not tied
     to any TCP stack, using the option --ss TIME_WAIT in combination with the
     --SS _s_t_a_c_k option results in ttccppddrroopp not dropping any TCP connection.

     The --ll flag may be given in addition to the --aa, --CC, --SS, or --ss options to
     list the tcpdrop invocation to drop all corresponding TCP connections one
     at a time.

     If none of the --aa, --CC, --SS, or --ss options are specified then only the con‐
     nection between the given local address _l_o_c_a_l_-_a_d_d_r_e_s_s, port _l_o_c_a_l_-_p_o_r_t,
     and the foreign address _f_o_r_e_i_g_n_-_a_d_d_r_e_s_s, port _f_o_r_e_i_g_n_-_p_o_r_t, will be
     dropped.

     Addresses and ports may be specified by name or numeric value.  Both IPv4
     and IPv6 address formats are supported.

     The addresses and ports may be separated by periods or colons instead of
     spaces.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The ttccppddrroopp utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     If a connection to httpd(8) is causing congestion on a network link, one
     can drop the TCP session in charge:

           # sockstat -c | grep httpd
           www      httpd      16525 3  tcp4 \
                   192.168.5.41:80      192.168.5.1:26747

     The following command will drop the connection:

           # tcpdrop 192.168.5.41 80 192.168.5.1 26747

     The following command will drop all connections but those to or from port
     22, the port used by sshd(8):

           # tcpdrop -l -a | grep -vw 22 | sh

     To drop all TCP connections using the new-reno congestion control algo‐
     rithm use:

           # tcpdrop -C new-reno

     The following command will drop all connections using the TCP stack rack:

           # tcpdrop -S rack

     To drop all TCP connections in the LAST_ACK state use:

           # tcpdrop -s LAST_ACK

     To drop all TCP connections using the congestion control algorithm new-
     reno and the TCP stack rack and being in the LAST_ACK state use:

           # tcpdrop -C new-reno -S rack -s LAST_ACK

